# Findings that are decisions rather than defects, recorded here so the ones
# left open are the ones that still need answering.
#
# Everything not listed stays reported. That is deliberate: encryption at rest
# on the shared volume, the EKS module's public endpoint, and open egress are
# real questions, and silencing them here would be answering them by default.

# AWS-0024 — point-in-time recovery on the DynamoDB seed table.
#
# The table holds one row per cluster naming which node formed the etcd
# cluster, written once at formation and never refreshed. It is a bootstrap
# hint, not a record: a joiner that finds it stale asks the surviving peers
# instead (see node-bootstrap.sh's find_live_peer). Restoring it to a past
# point would restore a pointer to a node that has since been replaced, which
# is worse than the empty table a fresh cluster starts from.
AWS-0024

# AWS-0025, AWS-0027 — customer-managed KMS keys for the table and the volume.
#
# These modules are meant to stand up a cluster in an account that has nothing
# else prepared, so they create no keys and depend on none. An operator with a
# key management policy sets it on the resources these produce; requiring one
# here would make the common case need a key before it can start.
AWS-0025
AWS-0027

# AWS-0066 — X-Ray tracing on the graceful-leave Lambda.
#
# The function runs once per scale-in and does one thing: remove the departing
# node from etcd's membership. What it did is in its CloudWatch log, and a
# trace across a single synchronous call adds a service dependency without
# adding an answer.
AWS-0066
